  "question": {
    "text": "If the angle $\\theta$ between the line $\\frac{x+1}{1}=\\frac{y-1}{2}=\\frac{z-2}{2}$ and the plane $2 x-y+\\sqrt{\\lambda} z+4=$ 0 is such that $\\sin \\theta=\\frac{1}{3}$ the value of $\\lambda$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "mfHSG",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{5}{3}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "2i6ba",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{-3}{5}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "g7x5i",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{3}{4}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "aEplk",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{-4}{3}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Angle between line and normal to plane is\n$\\cos \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\theta\\right)=\\frac{2-2+2 \\sqrt{\\lambda}}{3 \\times \\sqrt{5+\\lambda}}$ where $\\theta$ is angle between line \\& plane\n$$\n\\Rightarrow \\sin \\theta=\\frac{2 \\sqrt{\\lambda}}{3 \\sqrt{5+\\lambda}}=\\frac{1}{3}\n$$\n$\\Rightarrow \\lambda=\\frac{5}{3}$.",
    "image": null
  },
